## 3–5 Reusable Findings

1. **Proposal of a fourth line comprising external auditors and supervisors** (feeds S6): The paper extends the standard 3LoD model by proposing a "four lines of defence" model in which external auditors and supervisors — formally outside the organisation — are given a defined role within the institution's internal control system, on the basis of a "triangular" relationship among internal auditors, supervisors, and external auditors.

2. **Explicit risk of information-flow problems among the four actors** (feeds S6): While arguing the four-lines model enhances control systems through new responsibilities/relationships, the authors explicitly flag the risk that "new problems could be caused by inadequate information flows" among internal auditors, supervisors, and external auditors — a directly transferable caution for proposals to add external supervisors/auditors as a "fourth line" of AI oversight (e.g., third-party AI auditors under RBI FREE-AI).

3. **Internal control reform paired with corporate governance reform** (feeds S6): The paper argues internal-control-system reforms "should accompany corporate governance reforms" to improve risk-taking quality, and calls for "increasing cooperation… between the risk supervision committee and other parts of the firm" — links institutional-design (governance) reform to control-system (operational) reform, a structural argument applicable to why AI governance frameworks need both board-level and operational-level reform simultaneously.

4. **Misconduct-driven prioritisation of internal control reform** (feeds S6): The paper situates the four-lines proposal within a post-crisis context where "incidents have led to further prioritisation of governmental and supervisory agendas relating to the potential systemic implications of weak internal control systems" — provides a historical (2015, pre-AI) precedent for "incident-driven" regulatory escalation, comparable to how AI-incident events may drive future binding AI governance rules.

